The Hallia Metropolitan Conservatory, called the MetCon, is the great art museum of [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]], close to the [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Aurideon/index|Aurideon]] in the upscale centre of the city by the river. It holds the widest breadth of art in [[Geography/Calaria/index|Calaria]]: a four-story collection of hundreds of years' worth of work, where the culture Calaria has plundered from other countries is kept beside the country's own art.

The building is made entirely of glass and has a swooping shape that reaches out over the river that runs through the city. On the second, third and fourth floors parts of the floor are glass and look down into the water, which magical scrubbers keep clean for the view even though sewage is dumped in upstream. It is an architectural piece in its own right, with a small free parking garage at the entrance. Most of the staff follow [[Iridescent Host|the Host]], and the museum charges for its tours.

The big exhibit is a set of works by [[People/Paolo Barreto|Paolo Barreto]], a painter from [[Geography/Tormeré/index|Tormeré]] jailed for heresy. Among the pieces on display:

- *Joker World*, by the contemporary painter [[People/Kellum Quinn|Kellum Quinn]]. Most of the canvas is a detailed reddish-black smoke rising from a volcano, over red cracked clay terrain, a [[Phenomena/Joker World|Joker World]] Quinn came across in his travels. It is for sale at 64,000 gold, near the middle of the museum's prices.
- *Stillness Ever Takes*, by Beats in Time, the one piece in the whole museum drawn by a [[Phenomena/Hearts|Heart]].
- *The Six*, by Paolo Barreto, six abstract pieces for the six [[Divinity/Outer Gods/index|Outer Gods]], and Barreto's sculpture in the next room, a solid gold ball.
- An enclosed exhibit of art saved from [[Geography/Raelion/index|Raelion]], among it *Catastrophe Foretold*, which depicts the Raelish capital.
- *Fūlwinter Falls*, drawn by [[People/Estañion|Estañion]], a scholar of [[Geography/Lorandris/index|Lorandris]], and an untitled work bartered from somewhere further along the [[Infinite Horizon|horizon]] and carried home by Istrian explorers.

The museum has no cameras, but at least two security guards stand in every room at night, and alarm spells are laid on the pieces worth taking. Its one blot of shame is a con about twenty years old: someone passed themselves off as a famous artist, used the setting-up of their exhibit to walk in and swap several paintings for forgeries, and left with the originals. They were never caught.

The museum's guides send anyone asking after ursine art down the road to the Museum of Supernatural History, which keeps a whole area for bears.
